From 1ba7e29d1e240de6997598558e05a871afc06366 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 04:18:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: a Blu-ray playlist played directly goes on its
 own timeline

libbluray hands a playlist to the player as one transport stream, one clip
after the other, and each clip keeps its own timestamps. At a seamless join
(connection_condition 5 or 6: the cuts of a film with several versions) the
two clips are timed independently, so the timestamps step there by
IN(next) - OUT(previous): on M3GAN 2.0's 00801.mpls between -0.46 and
+0.35 s (SamuriHL's measurements). CDVDDemuxFFmpeg::ConvertTimestamp() only
takes one start time off, and CVideoPlayer::CheckContinuity() leaves alone
any step under a second, so a forward step is played as dead time (the
picture freezes, ActiveAE pads passthrough audio with pauses: TrueHD stops
for the length of the step and a receiver can lose its lock) and a backward
one as an overlap (pictures dropped, audio skipped later). The MAT packer
already carries TrueHD across the branch point (Kodi PR 28541, from LAV
Filters); what was missing is what LAV's splitter does: the timeline of the
title.

Each packet is now put on the playlist's timeline from the play item its
bytes were read in: its distance from that play item's IN time on the 33-bit
clock, plus where the play item starts in the title (BLURAY_CLIP_INFO's
in_time and start_time). CDVDInputStreamBluray counts what Read() returns and
notes where each play item begins (libbluray ends a read at the end of a clip,
so the bytes of the read that opens one are all of it; a seek starts afresh);
CDVDDemuxFFmpeg finds a packet's play item by its position. Pure parts in
BlurayTitleTime.h. Packets just before the start are put at it, as
ConvertTimestamp() does.

Only for a playlist played directly (not from the disc's menus, where the
timeline is the menus' and every play item reopens the streams), and not for
the base view of a 3D disc, whose dependent view CDVDDemuxBluray3D matches to
it on the clip's timeline. kodi.log tells each join with the step put away.

Tested on the host with discs made for it (two independently encoded clips,
MPEG-2 and TrueHD, the second at connection condition 5 with a step of +0.332
and -0.159 s) read by libbluray and FFmpeg's MPEG-TS demuxer as Kodi reads
them, through the player's own timestamp functions and the MAT packer: the
steps were there, uncorrected; now both streams go on without one, and the MAT
packer sees the branch point and loses nothing.

Design after matthane's for CoreELEC (the clip of each read byte, packets on
title time).
